      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & k \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & k \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (2)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))+((-2)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (2)-(k)\\cdot (3)))+((2)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-3)-(0)\\cdot (3)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (3k))+((-2)\\cdot (4-3k))+((2)\\cdot (-6))\\\\ &=(0)+(6k-8)+(-12)\\\\ &=6k-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-20&=0\\\\ 6k&=20\\\\ k&=\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{10}{3}.$",
