      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (3)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (0)))+((-2)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (3)-(0)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot (9))+((3)\\cdot (-3k))+((-2)\\cdot (3k))\\\\ &=(-9)+(-9k)+(-6k)\\\\ &=-15k-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15k-9&=0\\\\ -15k&=9\\\\ k&=-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{3}{5}.$",
