      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & k & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((-k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (2)))+((-k)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (-1)))+((3)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (2)-(-2)\\cdot (-1)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (-6))+((-k)\\cdot (3))+((3)\\cdot (-2))\\\\ &=(0)+(-3k)+(-6)\\\\ &=-3k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3k-6&=0\\\\ -3k&=6\\\\ k&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-2.$",
