      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & k \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (0)))+((2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (1)))+((k)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-3)\\cdot (1)))\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot (6))+((2)\\cdot (2))+((k)\\cdot (3))\\\\ &=(-18)+(4)+(3k)\\\\ &=3k-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-14&=0\\\\ 3k&=14\\\\ k&=\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{14}{3}.$",
