            "461b6578-874c-53dd-95a5-9b794cbced74"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & k & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (1)-(-3)\\cdot (0)))+((1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (1)-(-3)\\cdot (0)))+((-1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (0)-(k)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (k))+((1)\\cdot (-2))+((-1)\\cdot (0))\\\\ &=(2k)+(-2)+(0)\\\\ &=2k-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-2&=0\\\\ 2k&=2\\\\ k&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1.$",
