      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (-3)-(1)\\cdot (0)))+((-2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-3)-(1)\\cdot (2)))+((-1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(1)\\cdot (2)))\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot (-3))+((-2)\\cdot (-2))+((-1)\\cdot (-2))\\\\ &=(-3k)+(4)+(2)\\\\ &=6-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-3k&=0\\\\ -3k&=-6\\\\ k&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=2.$",
