      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ k & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ k & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (3)-(0)\\cdot (0)))+((-3)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (3)-(0)\\cdot (k)))+((-1)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (0)-(2)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot (6))+((-3)\\cdot (3))+((-1)\\cdot (-2k))\\\\ &=(-6)+(-9)+(2k)\\\\ &=2k-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-15&=0\\\\ 2k&=15\\\\ k&=\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{15}{2}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
