          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "a6e9ea11-d5e7-50ce-b090-49e313103faf"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & k & 1\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (1)-(-3)\\cdot (k)))+((1)\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (1)-(-3)\\cdot (0)))+((1)\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (3k))+((1)\\cdot (-3))+((1)\\cdot (-3k))\\\\ &=(6k)+(-3)+(-3k)\\\\ &=3k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-3&=0\\\\ 3k&=3\\\\ k&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
