      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-3)\\cdot (-2)))+((1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (0)-(-3)\\cdot (3)))+((k)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (3)))\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot (-6))+((1)\\cdot (9))+((k)\\cdot (-4))\\\\ &=(6)+(9)+(-4k)\\\\ &=15-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15-4k&=0\\\\ -4k&=-15\\\\ k&=\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{15}{4}.$",
