      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (2)-(-2)\\cdot (-3)))+((1)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (2)-(-2)\\cdot (0)))+((k)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (-3)-(0)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot (-6))+((1)\\cdot (2))+((k)\\cdot (-3))\\\\ &=(-18)+(2)+(-3k)\\\\ &=-3k-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3k-16&=0\\\\ -3k&=16\\\\ k&=-\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{16}{3}.$",
