      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & k & -3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ k & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (-3)-(0)\\cdot (k)))+((2)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (-3)-(0)\\cdot (2)))+((-2)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (k)-(-3)\\cdot (2)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (9))+((2)\\cdot (-3))+((-2)\\cdot (k+6))\\\\ &=(0)+(-6)+(-2k-12)\\\\ &=-2k-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2k-18&=0\\\\ -2k&=18\\\\ k&=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-9.$",
