      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))+((0)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (-1)))+((3)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-3)-(k)\\cdot (-1)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (0))+((0)\\cdot (0))+((3)\\cdot (k+6))\\\\ &=(0)+(0)+(3k+18)\\\\ &=3k+18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k+18&=0\\\\ 3k&=-18\\\\ k&=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-6.$",
