      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (0)-(-3)\\cdot (-3)))+((-3)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-3)\\cdot (-1)))+((2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-3)-(3)\\cdot (-1)))\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot (-9))+((-3)\\cdot (-3))+((2)\\cdot (3))\\\\ &=(-9k)+(9)+(6)\\\\ &=15-9k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15-9k&=0\\\\ -9k&=-15\\\\ k&=\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{5}{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
    },
