    "question": {
      ":question/id": "41768c52-d983-52eb-80c9-7e8eb573f1cb",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=\u27e8k,-3,3\u27e9$, $b=\u27e83,-1,0\u27e9$, and $c=\u27e80,1,2\u27e9$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d45d82b5-421f-5e0b-9304-7025a814db94",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "c012e0f8-a70b-5791-bfa8-9c800775b641",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{27}{2}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "c012e0f8-a70b-5791-bfa8-9c800775b641"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}k & -3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (2)-(0)\\cdot (1)))+((3)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (2)-(0)\\cdot (0)))+((3)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((k)\\cdot (-2))+((3)\\cdot (6))+((3)\\cdot (3))\\\\ &=(-2k)+(18)+(9)\\\\ &=27-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-2k&=0\\\\ -2k&=-27\\\\ k&=\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{27}{2}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
