      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & k \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (1)-(k)\\cdot (0)))+((1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (1)-(k)\\cdot (3)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (0)-(2)\\cdot (3)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (2))+((1)\\cdot (2-3k))+((-1)\\cdot (-6))\\\\ &=(0)+(2-3k)+(6)\\\\ &=8-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-3k&=0\\\\ -3k&=-8\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{3}.$",
