          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "80acbdfa-e7f1-5283-af75-a9521af66ee2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & k & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(1)\\cdot (k)))+((1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(1)\\cdot (2)))+((-1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (2)))\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot (-k))+((1)\\cdot (-2))+((-1)\\cdot (0))\\\\ &=(k)+(-2)+(0)\\\\ &=k-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2&=0\\\\ k&=2\\\\ k&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=2.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
