      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & k \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(k)\\cdot (0)))+((-1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(k)\\cdot (2)))+((3)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (0)-(-1)\\cdot (2)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (1))+((-1)\\cdot (2-2k))+((3)\\cdot (2))\\\\ &=(0)+(2k-2)+(6)\\\\ &=2k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+4&=0\\\\ 2k&=-4\\\\ k&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-2.$",
