      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((-k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (2)))+((-k)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (0)))+((-3)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (2)-(-1)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (2))+((-k)\\cdot (4))+((-3)\\cdot (-4))\\\\ &=(2)+(-4k)+(12)\\\\ &=14-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14-4k&=0\\\\ -4k&=-14\\\\ k&=\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
